---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { counts, formatDate, hostOf, meta, sources } from '../lib/content';
---

<Base
  path="/about/"
  title="About"
  description="What this site is, what it deliberately is not, how content gets added, and credit to every source it indexes."
>
  <div class="pagehead">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="pagehead__lede">{meta.positioning}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <section class="block">
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        A community index of Omarchy inspiration: {counts.setups} desks, {counts.themes} themes,
        {counts.plugins} plugins, and {counts.sources} other sites — filterable, searchable, with
        the official install command next to each record.
      </p>
      <p>
        The missing layer it adds is <em>posts plus desks plus "install this from here"</em>. The
        official catalogs collect what ships; nobody was collecting the workstations, the threads,
        and the write-ups alongside the command that reproduces them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>What it is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Not official.</strong> {meta.disclaimer}</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a replacement.</strong>
          <a href="https://omarchy.org/themes/" rel="noopener">omarchy.org/themes</a>,
          <a href="https://plugins.omarchy.org/" rel="noopener">plugins.omarchy.org</a>, and the
          community galleries stay canonical. This indexes them and links out.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a mirror.</strong> Every installable plugin in the marketplace is indexed
          here, each with its own page and its install command copied verbatim from the official
          catalog — but the marketplace stays canonical, and the plugin code, previews, and any
          security review stay upstream. It is a directory that points out, not a mirror of what
          the marketplace hosts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a security review.</strong> Plugins run as unsandboxed code. Read the repo.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No accounts, comments, or ratings.</strong> There is no back end and nothing to
          sign into; the pages are static files on a CDN. It does record some usage analytics —
          see <em>What it measures</em> below.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>What it measures</h2>
      <p>
        The site loads a hosted analytics script (Rybbit, from <code>app.rybbit.io</code>) on the
        canonical production domain only — never on a local build or a preview deploy. It records
        page views and a small set of events that show whether the index is doing its job:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Copies.</strong> Only when a copy actually succeeds, the first few words of the
          command (<code>omarchy theme set</code>, <code>omarchy plugin add</code>) — not the whole
          string, and never on a copy that failed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Outbound clicks.</strong> The host you leave for (<code>github.com</code>), not
          the full URL — that is already on the record in git.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Searches.</strong> The text you type — lowercased, whitespace-collapsed, cut to
          80 characters — plus how many results it found and which result you open. The box is
          free text, so treat it as public and do not type anything you would not put in a URL.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Filters.</strong> Which facet you toggle on a listing, and the result count.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every event also carries the path of the page it happened on. It is all best-effort — an
        ad blocker or a dropped request just means an event is never sent, and nothing on the page
        depends on it. The events are defined in
        <a href={meta.repo_url} rel="noopener">the repo</a> under
        <code>src/scripts/analytics.ts</code>, not in a dashboard setting.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>How content gets added</h2>
      <p>
        Content is files in git. Humans ship the site; a bot ships the content. Every record is a
        JSON object in <code>data/</code>, every hosted image is a WebP in
        <code>public/images/</code>, and both arrive as an ordinary commit to <code>main</code>.
        Cloudflare Pages rebuilds on push.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <code>node bot/ingest-sources.mjs …</code> pulls the public catalogs (themes, the
          plugin <code>catalog.json</code>, the hub's setup list).
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>node bot/validate.mjs</code> runs first in <code>npm run build</code>. Duplicate
          ids, missing fields, an install command that is not an <code>omarchy</code> command, or
          a hosted image path that does not exist all fail the build.
        </li>
        <li>
          Install commands are never composed by hand — they come from the upstream catalog or
          the verified table on <a href="/install/">the install page</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Last content update: <strong>{formatDate(meta.updated_at)}</strong>. Everything is on
        <a href={meta.repo_url} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Contribute or correct</h2>
      <p>
        You do not need git. Every detail page has <strong>Correct this record</strong> and
        <strong>Request removal</strong> links that open a GitHub issue with the record id
        already filled. Setups can also be sent with the
        <a href={`${meta.repo_url}/issues/new?template=setup-submission.yml`}>setup form</a>.
        A removal is reviewed; published ids are not deleted on a silent scrape.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Images</h2>
      <p>
        Where a record's image is mirrored here it is re-encoded to WebP, max edge 1200px,
        quality 80, metadata stripped, and the record says <code>image_hosted: true</code>.
        Bundled theme previews come from the MIT-licensed Omarchy repo; setup screenshots come
        from the MIT-licensed Omarchy Hub, credited on each card. Where an image is a listing
        thumbnail we do not host, the record says so and the detail page tells you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Any owner who wants a shot removed: open an issue and it goes, no argument.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Credit</h2>
      <p>Every site this one indexes, and would be nothing without:</p>
      <ul>
        {
          sources.map((source) => (
            <li>
              <a href={source.url} rel="noopener">
                {source.name}
              </a>{' '}
              — <span class="sourcecard__host">{hostOf(source.url)}</span>
              {source.blurb && <> · {source.blurb}</>}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        Omarchy itself is by <a href="https://omarchy.org/" rel="noopener">Omacom</a> and the
        people who send it patches.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
